00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  wednesday august 17th i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  have you ever typed your name into a  search engine if so you might have come  up with your social media profiles or  personal websites  or maybe you saw something a bit more  unnerving your email phone number or  even your home address  this can be upsetting it can feel like a  violation of privacy but it can also be  harmful depending on who finds that  information and how they use it  so what are the possible ramifications  of having your personal data be public  and how can you get that information  taken down  joining us to discuss this is our  reporter matty ellis thanks for joining  us maddie hi thank you for having me  let's start with some basics where is  this information like your home address  and phone being listed in the first  place why would anybody want to put it  up so  the best way to think about this is  think of a phone book or think of these  public records that before  you could access anyone could access  people actually wanted to be in the  phone book it was a way to connect with  friends find old friends give them a  call but because it wasn't digitized you  had to be really intentional about  actively using those sources other  examples are going to county offices and  requesting public records so there is  all this public information that is  relatively personal such as your home  address your phone number all of these  are recorded on utility bills property  taxes this all falls under the domain of  a public record what happens when this  is digitized is that the barrier to  accessing it goes way down so it can  just turn into a google search away so  what these sites like think of white  pages do for example is they aggregate  all this information from public records  so it is fair game it's all public but  they list it online and make it  available to be bought by anyone  this can potentially lead to some real  dangerous scenarios can you tell us a  little bit about those you know maybe  give us some examples  yes so the biggest example would be the  case of doxing which we see more and  more cases of that every day often  against politicians journalists but it  can happen to everyday people  and what docsin consists of is when your  personal information largely your home  address your phone number  often as well because of the way these  sites operate  the same personal information of family  members is posted publicly on sites like  social media with the intent to harass  threat and get other people to threaten  and harass that user again so some  notable examples of this is the recent  assassination attempt against supreme  court justice brent kavanaugh the person  who made that attempt admitted to  finding that information just on the  internet so that's a really extreme  example but i also spoke with people who  well they never  faced anyone physically threatening them  but they did have their home address  pictures of their apartments and their  buildings posted online just to create  that paranoia and that fear that some  danger might happen and it gets really  scary when they involve family members  that's what sources reported that to me  as well  okay so let's talk about some of the  steps then that you can take if you want  to get your personal information down  what is the first thing you should do  the first step is to assess your  immediate safety that's the number one  priority but if you are doing this  proactively one of the first things  someone trying to intimidate you might  do is just look it up in google and  google actually has tools to hide search  results that contain personal  information it's a new tool they just  expanded what is eligible to be hidden  in google search results to include  personal information contact information  address phone number all of that and in  the coming months google said they're  going to roll out a tool to make it even  easier so right now you have to go to  google you have to fill out a form  basically letting them know is this  being used for doxxing intent what  information do you want listed what's  the url that you want hidden from the  search results and why  but in the coming months there's gonna  be three little dots next to the posting  and you can click it it's an about this  result page and there will be an option  there to request removal so it really  expedites the process  it seems like google has a lot of leeway  though here i mean do they ever reject  these requests  yes so  some things that google said they will  take into consideration are  is this search result so is this url  contributing to  just the the free exchange of public  information so some examples of times  that they might reject requests to hide  a search result or if it's information  that comes from news coverage that's an  example of something that they likely  would not honor as well as if it comes  from an official government source  okay what about pages web pages that you  mentioned before like white pages if you  want to get it taken off of there not  just showing up in search  absolutely so that gets into the second  thing so what you can hide search  results in google if you manage to do  that successfully it doesn't actually  remove the content from the actual url  so people are able to access it other  ways other search engines if they know  the url they look it up directly so what  you have to do then is contact the  actual owner of the site and some quick  tools to do that are potentially to use  the database who is which can give you  some quick contact information but  specifically for these data broker sites  so the big ones mentioned white pages  spokayo  radaris these are sites that often have  opt-out requests the tricky part can be  finding these opt-out requests so what i  recommend doing is typing in the name of  the data broker and then opt-out that  should get you closer to the actual form  but essentially what you do is each  database is a little different but  basically usually it'll ask for the url  of your listing so you'll need to find  the location of your personal  information on their site save that url  submit that url to them and it's usually  just a few steps to say like i would  like to opt out  from having my information public what  about for people who just don't have the  time to go to all of these individual  sites and put in request forms it seems  like a lot of work to get something that  feels like yours off of places you don't  want it it is it is  a lot of work and it is a lifelong  effort of work so there are third-party  services that offer to do this for you  so one i spoke with the co-founder of  delete me and that service it's more  than 100 a month but what it does is  they make these requests on behalf of  the user so essentially all you do is  give delete me  your personal information you give it to  them so they know what to look for but  the delete me founder assured that what  they use proxy email addresses proxy  information to reach out to these sites  make these opt-out requests on behalf of  the customer without disclosing any more  information than needed there's also a  program from norton lifelock that does  this so there are third party services  out there  all right that's our reporter matty  ellis thanks so much for joining us  maddie thank you  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  you

